{
  "email": "hannah.lee@brightline.example",
  "reason": "KB-008 quick check: see if similar phishing messages were caught"
}

{
  "user_id": 12,
  "reason": "Precautionary check for unusual sign-ins given phishing attempt targeting new account setup"
}

{
  "user_id": 12,
  "reason": "Sign-ins show no MFA; note enrollment status for security team"
}

{
  "summary": "Reported: Hannah Lee (Payroll Specialist, Finance, user_id 12) received an email claiming to be from \"IT Support\" asking her to reply with her password to \"finish setting up her account\". She says she did NOT reply or disclose credentials, and reported no clicks or attachments. Sender address and exact subject not provided in ticket; user told to leave the message in her mailbox so security can pull headers. Checked: quarantine is empty (nothing similar caught, so the message got through filtering). Sign-in logs for 14 days look normal: all New York, US, corporate/residential IPs, usual hours, no failures, no hosting networks. Of note: account has must_change_password=true and password last set 2026-10-11, so the lure matched a real setup state (could be targeted, or the sender knew about onboarding). Also, user is NOT enrolled in Duo MFA and all sign-ins show mfa=none. That's a risk for a payroll user being targeted. Needed: security review of the sender/headers, block the sender org-wide if malicious, check whether other users got the same message, and confirm whether it came from an internal or compromised account. Recommend getting the user enrolled in Duo.",
  "reason": "KB-008: always escalate reported suspicious emails so a person reviews and blocks the sender"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, thanks for checking with us before replying. No, that email is not real. IT will never ask you for your password, by email or any other way. You did exactly the right thing by not replying. Please don't click any links in it, reply to it, or forward it to anyone. Just leave it in your mailbox for now so our security team can look at it. I've sent it to them, and they'll look into the sender and block it if needed. We checked your recent sign-ins and saw nothing unusual. If you did click anything or type your password anywhere, please tell us right away. Also, someone may contact you about setting up two-step sign-in, which adds extra protection to your account.",
  "internal_note": "KB-008 followed. User says she did not reply, click, or enter credentials, so KB-029 does not apply. Quarantine is empty: nothing similar was caught, so the message got past filtering. 14-day sign-in logs look normal (New York only, corporate or residential IPs, usual hours, no failures, no hosting networks). The account has must_change_password=true and a password set 2026-10-11, so the 'finish setting up your account' lure matched her real account state. That could mean targeting. User is NOT enrolled in Duo and all sign-ins show mfa=none, which is a risk for a payroll user. We don't have the sender address or subject yet; the message is left in her mailbox so security can pull the headers. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation 1) to review and block the sender, check other recipients and whether it came from an internal account, and get her enrolled in Duo.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
